To Japanese people, "ta-ta-ta-ta-taan"...

716
00:50:16,880 --> 00:50:18,980
だから、まあ、5文字くらいかな？
So, it's about five characters long, I guess?

717
00:50:18,980 --> 00:50:20,880
え？5？
Huh? Five?

718
00:50:20,880 --> 00:50:24,780
Like like sound, the sound that makes that pattern.
Like the sound that makes that pattern.

719
00:50:24,780 --> 00:50:26,580
A pattern?

720
00:50:26,580 --> 00:50:29,880
ヒント難しいね、文字数は。
It's hard to give a hint for this one.

721
00:50:29,880 --> 00:50:34,980
ちょっとタタタタターンもオノマトペと言われたら、もうごめんなさい。それは…
If you ask if "ta-ta-ta-ta-taan" is an onomatopoeia, I'm sorry. That's...

722
00:50:34,980 --> 00:50:37,480
えー、むずっ…
Huh, that's hard...

723
00:50:37,480 --> 00:50:41,880
もうタタタタタタターンしか思い出せない。
Now I can only think of "ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-taan".

724
00:50:41,880 --> 00:50:43,780
タタタタタタタ…
"Ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta..."

725
00:50:44,280 --> 00:50:46,080
んー、Wait...
Hm, wait...

726
00:50:46,080 --> 00:50:47,780
7文字か！
Ah, 7 characters!

727
00:50:47,780 --> 00:50:50,180
嘘でしょ、もう言ってるようなもん。
No way! That's basically giving it away!

728
00:50:50,180 --> 00:50:53,680
まあ、伸ばし棒が入ります、あと。最後の方に。
Well, it also includes a long vowel mark near the end.

729
00:50:53,680 --> 00:50:58,280
え！？ There is an elongated... Is it 7 characters?
Huh!? There is an elongated... Is it 7 characters?

730
00:50:58,280 --> 00:51:00,680
伸ばすと7文字ですね。
With the long vowel mark, it's 7 characters.

731
00:51:00,680 --> 00:51:02,180
ええー！？
Eeh!?

732
00:51:03,980 --> 00:51:06,680
あれろ、あげバーナーガストか。
What's that? Age-bana-gasuto?

733
00:51:07,780 --> 00:51:10,880
難しいね。
This is hard!

734
00:51:10,880 --> 00:51:15,580
この声、リズム自体はわかるんだけど、どう書いていいのかわからない。
I can picture the rhythm of the sound, but I don't know how to write it down.

735
00:51:15,580 --> 00:51:18,980
落ち着くね。タタタタターン、落ち着かないね。
It's relaxing. "Ta-ta-ta-ta-taan"—wait, isn't it unrelaxing?

736
00:51:18,980 --> 00:51:20,180
落ち着く？
Relaxing?

737
00:51:20,180 --> 00:51:23,780
この声を聞くと落ち着きますよ。「夏だな」って。
When you hear this sound, it feels relaxing. Like, "Ah, summer is here."

738
00:51:23,780 --> 00:51:25,480
えー？
Huh?

739
00:51:25,480 --> 00:51:28,180
That sound is relaxing to Japanese people?

740
00:51:28,180 --> 00:51:31,080
ええー？
Eeh?

741
00:51:31,080 --> 00:51:33,280
落ち着くよね？
It's relaxing, right?

742
00:51:35,080 --> 00:51:39,180
OK、じゃあ行きましょうか。イオピぴから。
Okay, let's go! Starting with Iofi-pi!

743
00:51:39,180 --> 00:51:44,280
いや、「ククククク」じゃないですか？セミバージョン2。
Isn't it "kukukuku"? Cicada Version 2!

744
00:51:44,280 --> 00:51:47,380
セミバージョン2！ククククク？
Cicada Version 2! "Kukukuku"?

745
00:51:47,380 --> 00:51:49,180
クククククで！
"Kukukuku" it is!

746
00:51:49,180 --> 00:51:50,680
オーケー、オーケー。じゃあ、こぼちゃん！
Okay, okay! Next, Kobo-chan!

747
00:51:50,680 --> 00:51:54,480
こぼ…
Kobo...

748
00:51:54,480 --> 00:51:58,380
Um, so...

749
00:51:58,380 --> 00:52:02,380
The previous one was "min-min", right?

750
00:52:02,380 --> 00:52:04,180
Uh-huh.

751
00:52:04,180 --> 00:52:07,780
And, uh, I heard...

752
00:52:07,780 --> 00:52:11,880
this is more than "min-min", so it's...

753
00:52:11,880 --> 00:52:14,680
a mating call like "meng-meng"!

754
00:52:14,680 --> 00:52:16,880
ちょっと待って！
Wait a second!

755
00:52:16,880 --> 00:52:20,580
そもそも、Why did you write "Mating Call"?
First of all, why did you write "Mating Call"?

756
00:52:20,580 --> 00:52:23,780
えっと、求愛の泣き声メンメンです。
Um, the mating call is "meng-meng".

757
00:52:23,780 --> 00:52:26,080
あー、求愛の泣き声なんだよね、実際。
Ah, it actually IS a mating call!

758
00:52:26,080 --> 00:52:29,880
あ、そうなの！？
Oh, really!?

759
00:52:29,880 --> 00:52:32,480
Why don't you write it IS a mating call?

760
00:52:32,480 --> 00:52:38,880
セミのオスがメスが来た時言ってる時の声だから、そこは正しい！
That's the sound male cicadas make when a female approaches, so that part is correct!

761
00:52:38,880 --> 00:52:40,980
正しい！求愛のね！
Correct! It's a courtship call!

762
00:52:40,980 --> 00:52:44,680
Oh my god!

763
00:52:44,680 --> 00:52:48,380
でもメンメンが正しいかどうかね。
But we'll see if "meng-meng" is correct or not.

764
00:52:48,380 --> 00:52:53,380
Ah, so "meng-meng", okay.

765
00:52:53,380 --> 00:52:55,280
じゃあ、ジジ！
Next, Gigi!

766
00:52:55,280 --> 00:52:58,280
Um, "ji", "ji", "ji"...

767
00:52:58,280 --> 00:53:00,680
上手！
So good!

768
00:53:00,680 --> 00:53:04,180
オーケー、「ジ」オッケーね！
Okay, "ji" is okay!

769
00:53:04,180 --> 00:53:05,780
じゃあ、べーちゃん！
Next, Bae-chan!

770
00:53:05,780 --> 00:53:07,780
え？なんか…
Huh? Something like...

771
00:53:07,780 --> 00:53:10,980
ポコポコピカ！ポコポコピカ！
"Poko-poko-pika! Poko-poko-pika!"

772
00:53:10,980 --> 00:53:14,180
ポケモンみたいだけど。
Sounds like a Pokémon though!

773
00:53:14,180 --> 00:53:16,880
ポケモンみたい！オーケー、ポコポコピカね！
Like a Pokémon! Okay, "Poko-poko-pika"!

774
00:53:16,880 --> 00:53:18,980
じゃあ、ラオーラ！
Next, Raora!

775
00:53:18,980 --> 00:53:22,080
Ah, you know, peanut... It's a cricket!
You know, it's a cricket!

776
00:53:22,080 --> 00:53:27,980
And a cricket sounds like "purin purin purin purin"!
And a cricket sounds like "purin-purin-purin-purin"!

777
00:53:27,980 --> 00:53:32,480
プリンプリン！かわいい！
"Purin-purin"! Cute!

778
00:53:32,480 --> 00:53:36,580
オーケー、オーケー！
Okay, okay!

779
00:53:36,580 --> 00:53:40,580
じゃあ、いきます！こちらの泣き声は…
Okay, here we go! This sound is...

780
00:53:40,580 --> 00:53:43,780
ツクツクボウシでございます！
Tsukutsukuboshi!

781
00:53:43,780 --> 00:53:47,080
ええーー！？
Ehhhh!?

782
00:53:47,080 --> 00:53:51,080
なぜ？なんで？Why?
Why? How? Why?

783
00:53:51,080 --> 00:53:54,080
ツクツクボウシ！ツクツクボウシ！
Tsukutsukuboshi! Tsukutsukuboshi!

784
00:53:54,080 --> 00:53:56,880
ツクツクボウシ！
Tsukutsukuboshi!

785
00:53:56,880 --> 00:54:00,680
So I should do this if I want to mating call someone?

786
00:54:00,680 --> 00:54:03,280
ちょっと待って、実際…
Wait a second, actually...

787
00:54:03,280 --> 00:54:06,880
Oh my god! Like that?

788
00:54:06,880 --> 00:54:10,580
ツクツクボウシ！ツクツクボウシ！
Tsukutsukuboshi! Tsukutsukuboshi!

789
00:54:10,580 --> 00:54:14,280
ヤバいやばいやばい！
Crazy! This is crazy!

790
00:54:14,280 --> 00:54:17,380
Yeah, buy! Yeah, buy! Shut up, please!
Yabai! Yabai! Shut up, please!

791
00:54:17,380 --> 00:54:21,080
学んでしまった！ Shut up! お願い、Shut up!
I learned something new! Shut up! Please, shut up!

792
00:54:21,080 --> 00:54:26,580
まあ、ツクツクボウシかツクツクホウシ、どちらでもOK。
Well, either "Tsukutsukuboshi" or "Tsukutsukuhoshi" is fine.

793
00:54:26,580 --> 00:54:33,380
どちらでもOKとなっております。
Either one is acceptable.

794
00:54:33,380 --> 00:54:37,480
ちょっとね、これセミは難しいですね。
Cicada sounds are pretty difficult, huh?

795
00:54:37,480 --> 00:54:39,780
Wow!

796
00:54:39,780 --> 00:54:45,380
セミの種類とかは関係ないんですか？あ、これはツクツクボウシっていうセミの鳴き声ですね、今のは。
Does the species of cicada not matter? Ah, that was the sound of a cicada called Tsukutsukuboshi just now.

797
00:54:45,380 --> 00:54:50,080
種類もツクツクボウシっていう名前のセミ。
The species name itself is Tsukutsukuboshi cicada.

798
00:54:50,080 --> 00:54:54,080
ちなみに他にも、さっきのがミンミンゼミだったでしょ？
By the way, earlier we had the Minmin cicada, right?

799
00:54:54,080 --> 00:54:59,980
アブラゼミっていうのが、ジジジジジッて鳴くのがアブラゼミ。
And Aburazemi is the one that goes "ji-ji-ji-ji-ji".

800
00:55:00,088 --> 00:55:01,388
セミだったり...
Like cicadas...

801
00:55:01,388 --> 00:55:03,088
あ、アブラゼミ！
Ah, Aburazemi!

802
00:55:03,088 --> 00:55:08,888
日本人だったら蝉の声を聞くだけで何の蝉かわかります。
If you're Japanese, just hearing a cicada tells you what kind it is.

803
00:55:08,888 --> 00:55:11,788
え！？ Wow!
What?! Wow!

804
00:55:11,788 --> 00:55:17,388
They can differentiate what kind of cicada it is depending on what it sounds like!

805
00:55:17,388 --> 00:55:19,488
There's different enchiladas...
There are different enchiladas...

806
00:55:19,488 --> 00:55:23,788
Yeah, so what we heard were two different enchiladas, Gigi.

807
00:55:23,788 --> 00:55:28,088
So you're saying that there is an enchilada that says "ji-ji"? Yeah!

808
00:55:28,088 --> 00:55:32,488
え、3種類って何の名前だったっけ？
Wait, what were the names of the three types again?

809
00:55:32,488 --> 00:55:35,788
ええと、ジジジはアブラゼミですね。
Um, "ji-ji-ji" is the Aburazemi, right?

810
00:55:35,788 --> 00:55:44,888
アブラゼミ！ はい。 Aburazemi... Aburazemi... Yes!
Aburazemi! Yes. Aburazemi... Aburazemi... Yes!

811
00:55:44,888 --> 00:55:51,088
Large brown cicada... Large brown cicada, yes!

812
00:55:51,088 --> 00:55:56,088
なるほど！ なるほど！
I see! I see!

813
00:55:56,088 --> 00:55:59,088
続いてラスト問題！
Moving on to the last question!

814
00:55:59,088 --> 00:56:05,088
これはめっちゃちょっと... ジャパニーズすぎる、難しいと思うんですけど...
This one is a bit... too Japanese, I think it's gonna be tough, but...

815
00:56:05,088 --> 00:56:11,788
これ解けたら、もういつ日本に来ても戦えます！
If you get this right, you can survive anytime you come to Japan!

816
00:56:11,788 --> 00:56:16,288
日本には独特な、これにしか使わないオノマトペがあります。
Japan has a unique onomatopoeia used only for this.

817
00:56:16,288 --> 00:56:26,588
桃が川から流れてくるオノマトペは何でしょう？
What is the onomatopoeia for a peach floating down a river?

818
00:56:30,888 --> 00:56:37,388
What is the onomatopoeia for when a peach comes floating down the river?

819
00:56:40,088 --> 00:56:46,588
桃太郎読んだことないんだったら、できないじゃないですか！ これ桃太郎！
If they haven't read Momotaro, they won't be able to answer! This is Momotaro!

820
00:56:47,088 --> 00:56:51,488
これ分かったら、もう日本に来ていただいていつでも...
If they get this right, they can come to Japan anytime...

821
00:56:51,488 --> 00:57:02,588
So what is the sound that a peach makes when it flows down the river?

822
00:57:02,588 --> 00:57:05,388
字が汚いよ！ ええと...
Your handwriting is messy! Um...

823
00:57:05,388 --> 00:57:14,388
Is it folklore? Yeah, you know Momotaro, the Peach Boy? Yeah, it's very specific.

824
00:57:14,388 --> 00:57:17,888
I can't... We gotta guess! We gotta guess!

825
00:57:17,888 --> 00:57:23,088
桃にしか使わないですね。りんごとかが代わりに流れてても使わない。
It's only used for peaches. Even if an apple floats down instead, it's not used.

826
00:57:23,088 --> 00:57:28,088
Only used for peaches? It's only peaches? My god.

827
00:57:28,588 --> 00:57:35,088
No, I'm sure this is gonna be really weird. 桃太郎読んだことないんだったら、分かんないやつ...
No, I'm sure this is gonna be really weird. If you haven't read Momotaro, you won't know it...

828
00:57:35,088 --> 00:57:39,088
桃太郎一回読んだんだよね？ お、じゃあ分かるじゃん。
You read Momotaro once, right? Oh, then you should know it.

829
00:57:39,088 --> 00:57:44,288
Maybe I know that legend point... oh, 'cause I watched...

830
00:57:44,288 --> 00:57:53,688
So if you read the actual book, it's written in the book. So if you haven't read it, you probably don't know.

831
00:57:54,088 --> 00:57:57,788
え、そんなことあるの！？
Eh, really?!

832
00:58:01,888 --> 00:58:07,788
なんか確か前にあの... 雑談配信でこれのことちょっと語った気がする。
I feel like I talked about this during a previous chat stream...

833
00:58:07,788 --> 00:58:13,788
そうなんだ！ そう。「なんでこうなったの？」って言われたら分かんないけど...
Oh, really?! Yeah. If asked "Why did it become like this?", I wouldn't know, but...

834
00:58:13,788 --> 00:58:16,888
すごい！ でも話すんだ。 昔話ね。
Wow! But you talk about it. Fairy tales, right.

835
00:58:19,888 --> 00:58:25,088
何だっけ？ これはね... 知らないと本当に当てられないと思うけど...
What was it? I think unless you know it, you really can't guess it...

836
00:58:25,088 --> 00:58:32,088
日本人だったら、ちっちゃい子からおじいちゃんおばあちゃんまでみんな分かるからね。
If you're Japanese, everyone from little kids to grandparents knows it.

837
00:58:32,088 --> 00:58:36,088
桃の川流れてる音なんだって。
They say it's the sound of a peach floating down a river.

838
00:58:37,088 --> 00:58:42,588
はい。じゃあ行きましょうか、最終問題。じゃあ、ゆぴぴどうでしょう？
Okay. Let's move to the final question. Yupipi, what's your answer?

839
00:58:42,588 --> 00:58:46,888
はい！ 「どんぶらこ」です。 はい、「どんぶらこ」！ 「どんぶらこ」、オッケー！
Yes! It's "Donburako". Yes, "Donburako"! "Donburako", okay!

840
00:58:47,088 --> 00:58:51,088
じゃあ、こぼちゃん！ お尻？ 「もんごもんご」！
Next, Kobo-chan! Butt? "Mongo mongo"!

841
00:58:51,088 --> 00:58:57,088
「もんごもんご」！？ 「もんごもんご」！
"Mongo mongo"?! "Mongo mongo"!

842
00:58:57,088 --> 00:59:03,088
かわいい！ お尻みたいに流れてるの？ うん！
Cute! Floating like a butt? Yeah!

843
00:59:03,088 --> 00:59:10,088
じゃあ、ジジ！ If I saw a huge peach floating in the water...
Okay, Gigi! If I saw a huge peach floating in the water...

844
00:59:10,088 --> 00:59:16,588
and it was like a really big peach and it was huge, and it was, you know, like a peach, I would go "Ah, wow! Dekai!"
and it was like a really big peach and it was huge, I would go "Ah, wow! Huge!"

845
00:59:16,588 --> 00:59:20,588
Like... めちゃめちゃJuicy! Dekai!
Like... Super juicy! Huge!

846
00:59:20,588 --> 00:59:26,088
おばあちゃんが「Juicy! Dekai!」って言うの？ 「Dekai!」ってなっちゃうの？
Would the grandma say "Juicy! Huge!"? Would she just go "Huge!"?

847
00:59:26,088 --> 00:59:33,088
「お尻 dekai!」「お尻 dekai!」 Oh my! 「桃 dekai!」
"Butt huge!" "Butt huge!" Oh my! "Peach huge!"

848
00:59:33,088 --> 00:59:36,088
じゃあ、ビッちゃん！
Next up, Bijou-chan!

849
00:59:36,088 --> 00:59:41,588
世長... 「ドンプシャー！」っていう...
Yonaga... "Donpusha!" or something...

850
00:59:41,588 --> 00:59:48,588
「ドンプシャー！」 水のね！ 水の音ね！
"Donpusha!" Water sound, right! Water sound!

851
00:59:48,588 --> 00:59:52,088
はい。じゃあ、続いてラオーラ！
Okay. Next up, Raora!

852
00:59:52,088 --> 01:00:01,888
Um, maybe it's "Puka-puka"? Ah, "Puka-puka"! "Puka-puka"! Cute!

853
01:00:01,888 --> 01:00:04,088
オッケー。じゃあ、正解行きましょう！
Okay. Then let's reveal the correct answer!

854
01:00:06,436 --> 01:00:10,036
どんぶらこ、どんぶらこですね。
It's "donburako, donburako," right?

855
01:00:10,036 --> 01:00:13,236
Oh my god! Oh my god!

856
01:00:13,236 --> 01:00:18,636
なぜです？なぜかは、そうやって本に書いてあるからね。
Why, you ask? Because that's what's written in books.

857
01:00:18,636 --> 01:00:20,636
へぇ〜
Huh~

858
01:00:20,636 --> 01:00:23,136
理由はない。え、ちなみに理由はない。
There's no reason. By the way, there's no reason.

859
01:00:23,136 --> 01:00:24,436
理由はない？
No reason?

860
01:00:24,436 --> 01:00:26,036
ない、ない、ないよ。
Nope, none at all.

861
01:00:26,036 --> 01:00:29,336
そう聴こえるのかな？
Maybe that's what it sounds like?

862
01:00:29,336 --> 01:00:31,436
で、あれだね。
And then...

863
01:00:31,436 --> 01:00:39,336
「ぷかぷか」は浮いているもの。水に浮いてたら「ぷかぷか」で合ってる。
"Puka-puka" is for floating things. If it's floating on water, "puka-puka" is correct.

864
01:00:39,336 --> 01:00:43,936
Oh, so if it's floating in the water, it would be "puka-puka".

865
01:00:43,936 --> 01:00:51,036
But 'cause it's coming down the stream, it's flowing down the stream...

866
01:00:51,036 --> 01:00:52,936
どんぶらこ、どんぶらこ。
Donburako, donburako.

867
01:00:52,936 --> 01:00:56,636
そう、桃が川から流れてくるときは「どんぶらこ」ですね。
Right, when a peach floats down a river, it's "donburako."

868
01:00:56,636 --> 01:01:02,236
And it has to be a peach? If it's an apple or anything else, it's not...?

869
01:01:02,236 --> 01:01:04,936
リンゴだったら「ぷかぷか」。
If it's an apple, it's "puka-puka."

870
01:01:08,636 --> 01:01:10,236
Oh my god!

871
01:01:10,236 --> 01:01:14,636
桃だけ？！ Peach only?!
Only peaches?! Peach only?!

872
01:01:14,636 --> 01:01:18,336
Oh my god!

873
01:01:18,336 --> 01:01:20,636
ということでね、皆さんも…
So anyway, everyone...

874
01:01:20,636 --> 01:01:25,636
Why Japan?! Why Japan?!

875
01:01:25,636 --> 01:01:28,336
難しいね、オノマトペ。
Onomatopoeia is difficult, isn't it?

876
01:01:28,336 --> 01:01:31,636
でも可愛かったのでね。
But it was cute.

877
01:01:31,636 --> 01:01:38,936
あ、ちなみにオノマトペではないんですけど、今日みんなに覚えていってほしい言葉があります。
Ah, by the way, it's not an onomatopoeia, but there's a phrase I want everyone to learn today.

878
01:01:38,936 --> 01:01:40,436
Oh, there's a phrase she wants us to learn.

879
01:01:40,436 --> 01:01:47,236
「全然分からない」っていうのを日本語では「ちんぷんかんぷん」って言いますね。
To say "I don't understand at all," in Japanese we say "chinpun kanpun."

880
01:01:47,236 --> 01:01:52,636
ちんぷんかんぷん！可愛い！
Chinpun kanpun! How cute!

881
01:01:52,636 --> 01:02:00,336
ちんぷんかんぷん！
Chinpun kanpun!

882
01:02:00,336 --> 01:02:08,636
そうそうそう。「全然分からない」…
Right, right. When you have no idea...

883
01:02:09,336 --> 01:02:14,636
When you have no idea of anything, this is the onomatopoeia you use.

884
01:02:14,636 --> 01:02:20,636
ちんぷんかんぷん！ちんぷんかんぷん！
Chinpun kanpun! Chinpun kanpun!

885
01:02:20,636 --> 01:02:24,936
I like that! I'm very scared of you using this phrase.

886
01:02:24,936 --> 01:02:27,636
Every time someone asks me something, "chinpun kanpun!"

887
01:02:27,636 --> 01:02:31,936
I'm just scared of you forgetting what the full phrase is...

888
01:02:31,936 --> 01:02:36,136
ちん…
Chin...

889
01:02:36,136 --> 01:02:40,636
残酷な言葉ですよ、「チン」ツンカンプン…
That becomes a dirty word! "Chin"-tsun-kanpun...

890
01:02:40,636 --> 01:02:45,136
はい、ということで…
Yes, so...

891
01:02:45,136 --> 01:02:51,136
Scared of using "chinpun kanpun"...

892
01:02:51,136 --> 01:02:56,636
全部覚えられないとき…
When you can't remember everything...

893
01:02:56,636 --> 01:03:00,636
The "chin" part that you can't remember is scary!

894
01:03:00,636 --> 01:03:05,136
覚えて、「ちんぷんかんぷん」。漢字もあるからね、「珍紛漢紛」って。
Remember "chinpun kanpun." It even has kanji, "珍紛漢紛."

895
01:03:05,136 --> 01:03:08,636
